Goal: main stages of the water cycle
- [5] - Water can be found in different states; the passage from one to the other is what characterizes the hydrological cycle, and this constant movement occurs with the aid of the Sun and gravity.
- The main stages of the water cycle are: evaporation, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, transpiration, and sublimation.
- Evaporation is the first stage: water goes from a liquid state into a gaseous state; in nature this occurs slowly and gradually and is especially influenced by temperature and solar irradiation, with higher temperature making evaporation faster.
- Condensation is the second stage: after evaporating and reaching a gaseous state, water vapor reaches the atmosphere and returns to its liquid form; clouds form as suspended water droplets come together, getting bigger and heavier.
- Precipitation: the water droplets that came together return to Earth’s surface as rain; this is the time for the release of rain.
- Infiltration: rain falls somewhere, whether on the Earth’s surface, in rivers, or seas, but infiltration concerns its absorption by the soil; this forms groundwater, aquifers, and other groundwater sources. The water absorbed by the soil also helps form natural “reserves” and irrigates plants through their roots; the excess is released during the perspiration phase.
- Transpiration, like evaporation, is the transformation of liquid water into vapor.
- Sublimation: water leaves the solid state into the gaseous state — it passes directly from ice to vapor; this may not be as noticeable in everyday life but is common in glaciers at the North Pole, and the water vapor produced is transferred to the atmosphere.
- [6] - The water cycle, also known as the hydrologic cycle, is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth.
- It involves the exchange of heat energy, which leads to changes in temperature and moisture levels in the atmosphere, oceans, and land.
- The water cycle is driven by solar energy and involves several stages including evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, and runoff.
- These stages are connected, and water can move from one stage to the next, eventually returning to oceans and other bodies of water where the cycle starts again.
- The 7 stages of the water cycle are: evaporation, condensation, sublimation, precipitation, transpiration, runoff, and infiltration.
- Evaporation: water at the surface turns into water vapor; water absorbs heat energy from the sun; oceans, seas, lakes, and rivers are the main source; water moves from hydrosphere to atmosphere; as water evaporates it reduces the temperature of those water bodies.
- Condensation: water vapor rises into the atmosphere; at high altitudes it changes into very tiny particles of ice/water droplets because of low temperature; these particles come close together and form clouds and fogs in the sky.
- Sublimation: ice directly converts into water vapor without converting into liquid water; this phenomenon accelerates when the temperature is low or pressure is high; main sources are the ice sheets of the North Pole and South Pole and ice caps on mountains; sublimation is slower than evaporation.
- Precipitation: clouds pour down as precipitation due to wind or temperature change; water droplets combine to make bigger droplets, so when the air cannot hold any more water it precipitates; at high altitudes the droplets lose their heat energy; droplets fall as rain, or as snow if below 0 degrees; water could also precipitate as drizzle, sleet, and hail; water enters lithosphere by landing upon the earth.
- Transpiration: some precipitated water is absorbed by the soil and enters transpiration; transpiration is similar to evaporation where liquid water is turned into water vapor by plants; roots absorb water and push it toward leaves where it is used for photosynthesis; extra water is moved out of leaves through stomata as water vapor; water enters the biosphere and exits into a gaseous phase.
- Runoff: water runs over the surface of the earth; when snow melts into water it also leads to runoff; as water runs over the ground it displaces the top soil and moves minerals along with the stream; runoff combines to form channels, rivers, and ends up into lakes, seas, and oceans; water enters the hydrosphere.
- Infiltration: any water that doesn't run directly to bodies of water or get quickly evaporated will be absorbed by plants and soil, where it may be driven deeper to the earth; water seeps down and increases the level of the ground water table; underground water tables typically provide pure clean water; infiltration is measured as inches of water-soaked by the soil per hour.
- [7] - The earth has a certain amount of water that moves around the earth in different states and phases.
- The first stage is evaporation: the sun heats water in oceans, lakes, and other bodies of water to the point that it turns into gas, and that gas evaporates into the air.
- Transpiration is similar to evaporation and occurs when plant leaves release water as water vapor into the air.
- Condensation is the third step: vapor in the air gets cold and changes back into a liquid; this is how a cloud forms and why you see foggy mirrors after a hot shower.
- When clouds have too much water and get too heavy, it rains; this is called precipitation, the final stage of the cycle.
- Then the whole cycle starts all over again.
- Review answer names evaporation, transpiration, condensation, and precipitation as stages of the water cycle.
- [8] - The water cycle is often taught as a simple, circular cycle of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.
- Although this can be a useful model, the reality of the cycle is much more complicated.
- Water has three phases (solid, liquid, and gas).
- In these three phases, water ties together the major parts of the Earth’s climate system — air, clouds, the ocean, lakes, vegetation, snowpack, and glaciers.

Goal: water cycle importance for life on Earth
- [9] - The water cycle is a continuous and vital movement across our planet and is essential to all life on Earth.
- It is a circular process involving evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.
- Understanding why the water cycle is important tells us how Earth functions and helps with increasing challenges on our blue planet.
- The water cycle is one of Earth’s most valuable systems.
- Its importance spans far beyond providing municipal water supplies to communities: it regulates climate, supports ecosystems and all life on Earth, enables human civilization, and shapes landscapes.
- Water covers more than half of our planet and is among Earth’s most useful and unique resources.
- Water can be solid, liquid, or gas under different conditions; this property allows the water cycle to work as the fundamental engine that can power all life.
- Without the water cycle, life would simply not exist.
- [10] - All life on Earth needs water.
- Water is so important that when searching for life on other planets, the first thing we look for is liquid water.
- Water changes to a gas and solid as part of the water cycle; without this happening, we wouldn’t have any fresh water.
- [11] - The water cycle is an extremely important process because it enables the availability of water for all living organisms and regulates weather patterns on our planet; if water didn’t naturally recycle itself, we would run out of clean water, which is essential to life.
- Climate changes include differences in temperature and precipitation patterns around the globe, bringing anticipated and unanticipated consequences that have a profound impact on people around the world.
- Changing extremes in precipitation and temperature are leading to a decline in species diversity, modification to ecosystems and animal habitats, as well as changing how some species interact with each other.
- Decision makers are using NASA Earth observations to reduce risks from natural disasters and build climate resilience, including index-based insurance products to protect low-income customers in Central America, especially the Dry Corridor.
- India is among the world’s most water-scarce countries, meaning it does not have enough freshwater resources to meet the needs of all the people who live there; mismanagement of water resources has largely contributed to the country’s limited water supplies.
- Growing competition over finite water resources will have serious implications for Indian farmers and India’s food security; more than 70% of India’s population rely on agriculture as a source of livelihood.
- Droughts and dry, arid conditions in Australia are conducive to wildfires; the 2019-2020 wildfire season was especially bad, with upwards of 46 million acres burned.
- Rainfall was scarce across much of the country in September, pushing the eastern and southern thirds of the country into drought conditions.
- Accurate and reliable precipitation records are crucial to understanding trends and variability and for water management resources, food security, ecological management, and weather, climate and hydrological forecasting.
- Monsoon rains provide important reservoirs of water that sustain human activities like agriculture and support the natural environment through replenishment of aquifers; however, too much rainfall routinely causes disasters in the region.
- The United States has seen drenching rains in the eastern half and persistent drought in the west; a visualization of rainfall data shows the contrast for the first half of 2015, including heavy rainfall in Northern Texas/Oklahoma and drought in Southern California.
- [12] - Water covers 70% of Earth’s surface and plays a vital role in everything from cellular function to climate.
- Water is vital for life on Earth, and it plays an essential role in regulating climate.
- When water evaporates, it absorbs heat and cools Earth’s surface; the vapor releases heat as it condenses into clouds, and water vapor traps the sun’s heat, creating a greenhouse effect that helps warm the planet—without it, Earth would be a ball of ice.
- On regional and local scales, the availability of water supports ecosystems and human activities such as agriculture and manufacturing.
- Changes to the water cycle can have profound consequences for society; major shifts in precipitation patterns likely led to the downfall of ancient civilizations around the world, from Angkor to Mesoamerica to the Indus Valley.
- Groundwater flows from land to sea, mixing with saltwater underground; though just 5% of ocean inflow, it can carry high chemical loads that impact coasts.
- The ocean water cycle is the marine branch of Earth’s water cycle; as the primary source of atmospheric moisture, it plays a crucial role in shaping global precipitation patterns and regulating climate.
- A warming climate increases evaporation and adds more water vapor to the atmosphere, and a warmer atmosphere can hold more water vapor, increasing downpours and leading to heavy rainfall events, catastrophic flooding, and more intense tropical cyclones.
- While some regions are inundated with rain, others experience drought as a warming climate alters atmospheric circulation patterns; prolonged drought can profoundly impact agriculture and contributes to extensive wildfires and loss of vegetation.
- Dams and reservoirs hinder the return flow of water to the ocean.
- Clear-cutting forests can decrease local precipitation and even change the location of developing storms.
- Rain over urban areas cannot soak into the ground when it encounters impermeable surfaces; instead it runs over surfaces and can overwhelm storm sewers, creating dangerous flash floods or more widespread flooding that can damage urban infrastructure.
- Air pollution affects the water cycle: water molecules attach to particles, condensing into tiny droplets; in polluted areas, clouds can contain many smaller droplets too small to fall as rain, which can limit precipitation.
- Pumping groundwater from aquifers for agriculture is removing water faster than it can replenish in many areas, leading to significant regional water shortages.

Goal: real example of water cycle disruption
- [13] - One example of the human impacts on the water cycle is in North China, which is experiencing an ongoing drought; vegetation in many areas continues to thrive, partially because producers continue to irrigate their land by pumping more water from groundwater storage.
- Such interrelated human interventions often lead to complex effects on other water cycle variables, such as evapotranspiration and runoff.
- The global water cycle is shifting in unprecedented ways, and the majority of those shifts are driven by activities such as agriculture and could have impacts on ecosystems and water management, especially in certain regions.
- Human intervention in the global water cycle is more significant than previously thought.
- The study focused on three kinds of shifts or changes in the cycle: a trend, such as a decrease in water in a groundwater reservoir; a shift in seasonality, like the typical growing season starting earlier or earlier snowmelt; and a change in extreme events, like “100-year floods” happening more frequently.
- [14] - The Water Project works in Kenya, Sierra Leone, and Uganda, where water cycle disruptions have real daily consequences.
- “When precipitation becomes unreliable”
- The water cycle works continuously across the planet, but it doesn’t deliver water equally; when something disrupts the cycle — drought, pollution, conflict, infrastructure collapse — entire communities lose access to clean water.
- A side-by-side illustration compares a healthy water cycle over a thriving community with a full well and green landscape to a broken water cycle over the same community during water scarcity with a dry well and cracked earth.
- Understanding the water cycle matters because every community on Earth depends on it for fresh water: when the cycle works, rain falls, rivers flow, wells fill, and people have water to drink; when any part of the cycle is disrupted — by drought, pollution, or climate change — communities lose access to water they need to survive.
- [15] - Climate change has disrupted the water cycle’s delicate balance, upsetting how water circulates between the ground, oceans and atmosphere; the events of 2023 showed consequences from extreme precipitation and flooding to drought and contaminated water supplies in almost every part of the U.S.
- As the planet warms, the water cycle is expected to be increasingly stretched, warped and broken.
- In 2023, extreme precipitation, likely supercharged by climate change, hammered nearly every corner of the United States; for every degree of warming in Fahrenheit, the atmosphere can hold about 3%-4% more moisture, and global temperatures in 2023 were 2.43 degrees higher than in preindustrial times, meaning today’s storms can deliver a stronger punch.
