## main stages of the water cycle

- Water can be found in different states; the passage from one to the other is what characterizes the hydrological cycle, and this constant movement occurs with the aid of the Sun and gravity. [5]
- The main stages of the water cycle are: evaporation, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, transpiration, and sublimation. [5]
- Evaporation is the first stage: water goes from a liquid state into a gaseous state; in nature this occurs slowly and gradually and is especially influenced by temperature and solar irradiation, with higher temperature making evaporation faster. [5]
- Condensation is the second stage: after evaporating and reaching a gaseous state, water vapor reaches the atmosphere and returns to its liquid form; clouds form as suspended water droplets come together, getting bigger and heavier. [5]
- Precipitation: the water droplets that came together return to Earth's surface as rain; this is the time for the release of rain. [5]
- Infiltration: rain falls somewhere, whether on the Earth's surface, in rivers, or seas, but infiltration concerns its absorption by the soil; this forms groundwater, aquifers, and other groundwater sources. The water absorbed by the soil also helps form natural "reserves" and irrigates plants through their roots; the excess is released during the perspiration phase. [5]
- Transpiration, like evaporation, is the transformation of liquid water into vapor. [5]
- Sublimation: water leaves the solid state into the gaseous state — it passes directly from ice to vapor; this may not be as noticeable in everyday life but is common in glaciers at the North Pole, and the water vapor produced is transferred to the atmosphere. [5]
- The water cycle, also known as the hydrologic cycle, is the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. [6]
- It involves the exchange of heat energy, which leads to changes in temperature and moisture levels in the atmosphere, oceans, and land. [6]
- The water cycle is driven by solar energy and involves several stages including evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, infiltration, and runoff. [6]
- These stages are connected, and water can move from one stage to the next, eventually returning to oceans and other bodies of water where the cycle starts again. [6]
- The 7 stages of the water cycle are: evaporation, condensation, sublimation, precipitation, transpiration, runoff, and infiltration. [6]
- Evaporation: water at the surface turns into water vapor; water absorbs heat energy from the sun; oceans, seas, lakes, and rivers are the main source; water moves from hydrosphere to atmosphere; as water evaporates it reduces the temperature of those water bodies. [6]
- Condensation: water vapor rises into the atmosphere; at high altitudes it changes into very tiny particles of ice/water droplets because of low temperature; these particles come close together and form clouds and fogs in the sky. [6]
- Sublimation: ice directly converts into water vapor without converting into liquid water; this phenomenon accelerates when the temperature is low or pressure is high; main sources are the ice sheets of the North Pole and South Pole and ice caps on mountains; sublimation is slower than evaporation. [6]
- Precipitation: clouds pour down as precipitation due to wind or temperature change; water droplets combine to make bigger droplets, so when the air cannot hold any more water it precipitates; at high altitudes the droplets lose their heat energy; droplets fall as rain, or as snow if below 0 degrees; water could also precipitate as drizzle, sleet, and hail; water enters lithosphere by landing upon the earth. [6]
- Transpiration: some precipitated water is absorbed by the soil and enters transpiration; transpiration is similar to evaporation where liquid water is turned into water vapor by plants; roots absorb water and push it toward leaves where it is used for photosynthesis; extra water is moved out of leaves through stomata as water vapor; water enters the biosphere and exits into a gaseous phase. [6]
- Runoff: water runs over the surface of the earth; when snow melts into water it also leads to runoff; as water runs over the ground it displaces the top soil and moves minerals along with the stream; runoff combines to form channels, rivers, and ends up into lakes, seas, and oceans; water enters the hydrosphere. [6]
- Infiltration: any water that doesn't run directly to bodies of water or get quickly evaporated will be absorbed by plants and soil, where it may be driven deeper to the earth; water seeps down and increases the level of the ground water table; underground water tables typically provide pure clean water; infiltration is measured as inches of water-soaked by the soil per hour. [6]
- The earth has a certain amount of water that moves around the earth in different states and phases. [7]
- The first stage is evaporation: the sun heats water in oceans, lakes, and other bodies of water to the point that it turns into gas, and that gas evaporates into the air. [7]
- Transpiration is similar to evaporation and occurs when plant leaves release water as water vapor into the air. [7]
- Condensation is the third step: vapor in the air gets cold and changes back into a liquid; this is how a cloud forms and why you see foggy mirrors after a hot shower. [7]
- When clouds have too much water and get too heavy, it rains; this is called precipitation, the final stage of the cycle. [7]
- Then the whole cycle starts all over again. [7]
- Review answer names evaporation, transpiration, condensation, and precipitation as stages of the water cycle. [7]
- The water cycle is often taught as a simple, circular cycle of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation. [8]
- Although this can be a useful model, the reality of the cycle is much more complicated. [8]
- Water has three phases (solid, liquid, and gas). [8]
