- The Water Project works in Kenya, Sierra Leone, and Uganda, where water cycle disruptions have real daily consequences. [14]
- "When precipitation becomes unreliable" [14]
- The water cycle works continuously across the planet, but it doesn't deliver water equally; when something disrupts the cycle — drought, pollution, conflict, infrastructure collapse — entire communities lose access to clean water. [14]
- A side-by-side illustration compares a healthy water cycle over a thriving community with a full well and green landscape to a broken water cycle over the same community during water scarcity with a dry well and cracked earth. [14]
- Understanding the water cycle matters because every community on Earth depends on it for fresh water: when the cycle works, rain falls, rivers flow, wells fill, and people have water to drink; when any part of the cycle is disrupted — by drought, pollution, or climate change — communities lose access to water they need to survive. [14]
- Climate change has disrupted the water cycle's delicate balance, upsetting how water circulates between the ground, oceans and atmosphere; the events of 2023 showed consequences from extreme precipitation and flooding to drought and contaminated water supplies in almost every part of the U.S. [15]
- As the planet warms, the water cycle is expected to be increasingly stretched, warped and broken. [15]
- In 2023, extreme precipitation, likely supercharged by climate change, hammered nearly every corner of the United States; for every degree of warming in Fahrenheit, the atmosphere can hold about 3%-4% more moisture, and global temperatures in 2023 were 2.43 degrees higher than in preindustrial times, meaning today's storms can deliver a stronger punch. [15]
- Vermont: intense rainfall in July caused flash flooding that nearly breached a dam in Montpelier and left streets flooded. [15]
- New York City: in September, 7 inches of rain fell in 24 hours in some locations, submerging cars and city buses and shuttering rail travel; consequences intensified where storm drains and subway tunnels are in disrepair or were built for a more gentle climate. [15]
- Climate change is changing hurricane behavior to produce more extreme rainfall: hurricanes are more likely to intensify rapidly because they feed on warming waters near shore, and when they make landfall, climate change increases the probability they will stall and dump incredible amounts of rain. [15]
- Hurricane Idalia: when it approached the Florida shoreline in late August, its wind speeds rose by 55 mph in just 24 hours and it strengthened from a Category 1 to a Category 4 storm; it dropped more than 13 inches of rain on Holly Hill, South Carolina. [15]
- Hotter temperatures are increasing evaporation and transpiration in some areas, making drought more likely and stressing plants, evident during a summer of extreme heat in 2023; climate change makes droughts more frequent, more severe and longer-lasting. [15]
- Persistent drought dropped water levels in the Mississippi River to historic lows in 2023, allowing saltwater from the Gulf of Mexico to push upstream and contaminate urban drinking water supplies. [15]
- Drought in Hawaii exacerbated wildfire risk by drying out vegetation and making nonnative grasses on Maui a "ticking time bomb"; a catastrophic wildfire whipped up by hurricane-strength winds tore through Lahaina in August 2023, killing 101 people. [15]
- In the Midwest and South, drought hung over the region from spring to fall, making it the most costly natural disaster in 2023 — at $14.5 billion. [15]
- Climate change is shifting the pattern and timing of runoff, particularly in mountainous parts of the U.S., which can cause rivers to run at extreme highs — and lows. [15]
- Alaska: temperatures warmed about twice the global average, a dam of glacial ice burst, allowing a massive pulse of floodwater to flow downstream, where it ripped out trees and flooded neighborhoods near Juneau; the event would not have happened if not for climate change and glacial retreat, according to NOAA. [15]
- Pacific Northwest: warm spring and summer temperatures hastened the region's melt-out, leaving the water supply short in fall and straining the region's capacity to generate hydropower. [15]
- Water supply is dwindling in Western states, on the Great Plains and in some parts of the Midwest; years of overuse — in part because of rising temperatures and drought — are leading farmers to consume unsustainable amounts of stored groundwater and pushing some aquifers to the brink. [15]
- California: extreme rainfall in 2023, as more than a dozen atmospheric-river storms battered the state, likely intensified by climate change, relieved a drought and blanketed the state in 2 to 3 times as much snow as usual, but made only a dent in the state's overall groundwater deficit after seasons of drought, and groundwater levels remained lower than after a previous four-year drought ended in 2016. [15]
- Several states reached an agreement over cuts to their use of the Colorado River, where reservoirs were holding just 43% of what they could store at year's end, even after a heavy snow year. [15]
- There is considerable evidence that humans are responsible for disruptions and changes to local and global water cycles. [16]
- Human activities that can impact the water cycle include damming rivers for hydroelectricity, using water for farming, deforestation and the burning of fossil fuels. [16]
- Different countries use different amounts of water, but we all tend to use them in the same ways, and some of these actions can impact the water cycle – generating hydroelectricity, irrigation, deforestation and the greenhouse effect, as well as motor vehicle use and animal farming. [16]
- Hydroelectricity: Most of New Zealand's electricity is generated using hydro dams; this is a non-polluting renewable way to generate electricity, but it does have environmental impacts – especially when mismanaged. [16]
- Clyde dam in the South Island uses water to generate electricity. It is New Zealand's third largest hydro dam. [16]
- Rivers must be dammed, which can affect the function of the river both upstream and downstream – lakes are usually formed from water accumulating above the dam and a build-up of silt can occur, while the amount of water is reduced further downstream. [16]
- This can be problematic for plants and animals that may find themselves with too much or too little water, and migrating fish cannot get through the dams. [16]
- Seriously mismanaged dams can result in droughts downstream, with smaller streams completely drying up, leaving areas of unwatered land; people then have to look at ways of getting more water into these dry areas. [16]
- Irrigation removes water from its natural source and often causes leaching and run-off where it is used. [16]
- This removal of nutrients results in farmers using more fertilisers to keep their pastures productive while the waterways become polluted. [16]
- Another problem is that salt is brought up from lower levels (salination). [16]
- The removal of trees (deforestation) is having a major impact on the water cycle, as local and global climates change. [16]
- Normally, trees release water vapour when they transpire, producing a localised humidity; this water vapour then evaporates into the atmosphere where it accumulates before precipitating back to the Earth as rain, sleet or snow. [16]
- Deforestation in one area can therefore affect the weather in another area because if trees are cut down, there is less water to be evaporated into the atmosphere and subsequently less rain. [16]
- At a local level, the land becomes drier and less stable. When it rains, instead of the water being soaked up, there is increased run-off and leaching. [16]
- Areas can become more prone to both droughts and flooding, impacting on plants and animals, and also humans living near deforested areas. [16]
- Human activity such as the burning of fossil fuels has an effect on the overall increase of the Earth's temperature. [16]
- Raising the Earth's temperature means that there is an increase of evaporation, melting of land and sea ice, and impacts on other processes of the water cycle that adversely affect the climate on Earth. [16]

## drought as water cycle disruption example

- Drought is an extended period of unusually dry weather when there is not enough rain. [17]
- The lack of precipitation can cause a variety of problems for local communities, including damage to crops and a shortage of drinking water. [17]
- Drought is defined depending on the average amount of precipitation that an area is accustomed to receiving; significantly less rain may lead to water shortages and a drought may be declared. [17]
- Most droughts occur when regular weather patterns are interrupted, causing disruption to the water cycle: changes in atmospheric circulation patterns can cause storm tracks to be stalled for months or years, dramatically impacting amounts of precipitation, and changes in wind patterns can disrupt how moisture is absorbed in various regions. [17]
- El Niño is a weather event where the surface water in the Pacific Ocean along the central South American coast rises in temperature; these warmer waters alter storm patterns and are associated with droughts in Indonesia, Australia, and northeastern South America. [17]
- La Niña is the counterpart to El Niño, when the surface water in the Pacific Ocean along the coast of South America decreases in temperature; the cooler waters affect storm patterns by contributing to drier-than-normal conditions in parts of North and South America. [17]
- Two of the most devastating droughts in U.S. history—the 1930s Dust Bowl and the 1988 drought in the Midwest—are associated with the effects of La Niña. [17]
- There is debate about the connection between drought and global warming; a 2013 NASA study predicts warmer worldwide temperatures will mean increased rainfall in some parts of the world and decreased rainfall in others, leading to both more flooding and more droughts worldwide, while other scientists believe global warming will create a wetter climate around the world. [17]
- Drought is a naturally occurring part of the weather cycle and cannot be prevented, but human activity can influence the effects that drought has on a region. [17]
- Modern agricultural practices may make land more vulnerable to drought; over-farming and overgrazing can lead to soil being compacted and unable to hold water, and as the soil becomes drier, it is vulnerable to erosion, leading to desertification. [17]
- Increased drought conditions in Kenya have been attributed to deforestation and other human activities; trees help bring precipitation into the ground and prevent soil erosion, but forest clearing for farming and logging led to drought conditions affecting 10 million people. [17]
- Analyzing paleoclimatic data shows that severe and extended droughts are an inevitable part of natural climate cycles. [17]
- The water cycle has become increasingly erratic and extreme, swinging between deluge and drought, according to a new WMO report; it highlights the cascading impacts of too much or too little water on economies and society. [18]
- Only about one-third of the global river basins had "normal" conditions in 2024; the rest were either above or below normal – the sixth consecutive year of clear imbalance. [18]
- The Amazon Basin and other parts of South America, as well as southern Africa, were gripped by severe drought in 2024, whilst there were wetter-than-normal conditions in central, western and eastern Africa, parts of Asia and Central Europe. [18]
- 2024 was the hottest year on record and began with an El Niño event which impacted major river basins; it contributed to droughts in northern South America and the Amazon Basin and southern Africa. [18]
- In the past six years, only about one-third of the global river catchment area had normal discharge compared to the 1991-2020 average; two thirds had too much or too little water – reflecting the increasingly erratic hydrological cycle. [18]
- There was much below-normal discharge across key river basins including the Amazon, São Francisco, Paraná, and Orinoco in South America, and the Zambezi, Limpopo, Okavango, Orange basins in southern Africa. [18]
- Reservoir inflows, groundwater, soil moisture and evapotranspiration trends showed regional contrasts, with recharge in wetter areas such as parts of Europe and India, but persistent deficits in parts of Africa, the Americas, and Australia; over-extraction of groundwater continued to reduce future water availability. Only 38% of wells had normal levels – the rest were too much or too little. [18]
- Brazil experienced simultaneous extremes, with catastrophic flooding in the south and continuation of the 2023 drought in the Amazon basin, affecting 59% of the country's territory. [18]
- An estimated 3.6 billion people face inadequate access to water at least a month per year, expected to increase to more than 5 billion by 2050, and the world is falling far short of Sustainable Development Goal 6 on water and sanitation. [18]
- Many small-glacier regions have already reached or are about to pass the so-called peak water point – when a glacier's melting reaches its maximum annual runoff, after which this decreases due to glacier shrinkage. [18]
- Human activity damages ecosystems, disrupting the water cycle; climate change worsens these pressures, and extreme weather events such as floods and droughts are becoming more frequent. [19]
- In the spring, melting snow feeds streams and rivers to provide a source of water during hot summer months; rising temperatures mean more rain rather than snow, the little snow there is evaporates instead of flowing downstream, and less snow means less water during hot periods. [19]
- With more water in the air than on the ground, the land begins to dry out; wet ground absorbs water much better than dry ground does. [19]
- When it finally rains after long periods of drought, the water washes away rather than being absorbed into the ground, causing flash floods; the longer the drought, the more water is needed to revive the land. [19]
- Climate change and human activity speed up the water cycle: more evaporation, more rain, more drought, and less water for us and all other life that depends on it. [19]
- Pollution and unsustainable land practices impact water systems; wetlands are drained, rivers and floodplains are artificially disrupted, rerouted, and built over; this further accelerates water scarcity. [19]
- Large-scale pumping of groundwater has depleted aquifers that stored water for thousands of years. [19]
- The most visible sign of our disrupted water cycle is when rivers diverted for agriculture simply dry up. [19]
- To restore the water cycle, slow freshwater's journey from rain back to the oceans and protect and restore nature to bring back the sponge-like ability of landscapes to hold water, undoing damage like draining wetlands and sealing soil. [19]
- Replenish groundwater by inundating farmland with winter floodwater so it seeps through the soil layer into the aquifer below. [19]
- Soils can hold eight times more water than all the rivers combined; reducing tillage and planting more cover crops helps soil store more water. [19]
- Restore wetlands and use farming techniques that conserve water and protect and rehabilitate the soil to maintain and recover the land's ability to absorb, purify and hold water. [19]
- Urban designers can use permeable materials for roads and sidewalks to allow water to soak into the earth, replenish groundwater, and gradually flow back into rivers and streams. [19]

